---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="angle-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="angle" from="deg" to="rad" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert an angle</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type the angle into either field. The same control converts degrees to radians and radians to degrees.</li>
      <li>Pick the units. The first group has degrees, radians, gradians and turns. The second has the sub-degree units of astronomy, optics and ballistics.</li>
      <li>The formula panel shows the exact factor, and the chart below lists the standard angles.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Degrees and radians</h2>
    <p>
      A full turn is 360 degrees or 2π radians, so the conversion factor is π/180, about
      0.01745329252. In the other direction, one radian is 180/π degrees, or 57.29577951°.
    </p>
    <p>
      Radians are the natural unit for calculus and circular motion: an arc of one radian on a unit
      circle has length one. For this reason, the derivative of sin(x) is cos(x); in degrees, a factor
      of π/180 appears. The trigonometric functions of all programming languages expect radians, which
      is the most common practical reason to convert.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Degrees</th><th>Radians (exact)</th><th>Radians (decimal)</th><th>Gradians</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0°</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30°</td><td>π/6</td><td>0.5235987756</td><td>33.333</td></tr>
          <tr><td>45°</td><td>π/4</td><td>0.7853981634</td><td>50</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60°</td><td>π/3</td><td>1.047197551</td><td>66.667</td></tr>
          <tr><td>90°</td><td>π/2</td><td>1.570796327</td><td>100</td></tr>
          <tr><td>180°</td><td>π</td><td>3.141592654</td><td>200</td></tr>
          <tr><td>270°</td><td>3π/2</td><td>4.712388980</td><td>300</td></tr>
          <tr><td>360°</td><td>2π</td><td>6.283185307</td><td>400</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Arcminutes, arcseconds and coordinates</h2>
    <p>
      Degrees divide by 60, like hours: one degree is 60 arcminutes, and one arcminute is 60
      arcseconds. Latitude and longitude are often written this way (51° 28′ 38″ N). To convert to
      decimal degrees, divide the minutes by 60 and the seconds by 3,600, then add them.
    </p>
    <p>
      For scale, an arcsecond is about the angle of a small coin seen from five kilometres, and one
      arcsecond of latitude is about 31 metres on the ground. Astronomers often work in
      milliarcseconds; the Gaia spacecraft measures stellar positions to a few tens of
      microarcseconds.
    </p>

    <h2>Milliradians, mils and shooting</h2>
    <p>
      A milliradian is a thousandth of a radian. At 100 metres, one milliradian covers almost exactly
      10 centimetres, and at 1,000 metres one metre. Range and holdover estimates are then a matter of
      moving a decimal point.
    </p>
    <p>
      The NATO mil approximates the same idea: it divides the circle into 6,400 equal parts instead of
      6,283.19. This gives 0.05625° per mil, against 0.0572958° for a true milliradian, a difference of
      about 2 %. Artillery uses the 6,400 version; most rifle scopes use true milliradians. The unit
      list has both, with separate labels, because mixing them at distance puts rounds well off
      target.
    </p>

    <h2>Gradians, and why your calculator has a DRG key</h2>
    <p>
      The gradian divides a right angle into 100 parts, so a full turn is 400 gon. It came from the
      decimalisation of the French Revolution, which also gave the metre. It is still used in European
      surveying and in the "GRAD" mode of scientific calculators. If your trigonometry answers are
      wrong by a constant factor, first check whether the calculator is in DEG, RAD or GRAD mode.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
